d love have me do today  and then the heart starts to answer  and you know some of it's tough that we  have to attend to this and do that  but what would love have me do today  what we're talking about is listening  and trusting  our own intuition and trusting our own  heart  to guide us  i have a little passage to read  which are instructions from  a thousand years ago or a long time ago  by this  tibetan  master meditation master teacher named  atisha  and here they are it's really what we've  been talking about and here's how we  talked about it he said advice it  teaches advice  you know  he said consider all experience  to be dreams  we take it all so seriously but there's  this vastness of the galaxies and  millions of people being born and dying  and  peace coming and war coming and joy and  sorrow and gain and loss and  consider it all to be  dreams  be grateful to everyone  thank you to everyone even the ones that  make  difficulty don't be swayed by outer  circumstances  don't brood over the faults of others  i love this one because it's so easy to  judge the others  explore the timeless nature of  consciousness  let suffering teach you compassion  at all times rely on a joyful mind  and the last is don't expect a standing  ovation  it's not a performance it's really  living your own life becoming the buddha  that you are the wise one the  wise woman or the wise man the sage  really embodying that that you just  found in yourself with your friend with  that kind of joy  that's both  able to accomplish things  but able to do it in a way that plants  seeds of well-being and adventure and  caring  thank you jack and i want to bring up  also one quote that you shared with me  long ago this is quite a few years ago i  think it was in our first conversation  on the podcast and this is i believe a  quote you can correct me from wrong  from  ryokan  ryokan  who is a quiet and unconventional soto  zen buddhist monk i'm reading for  wikipedia who lived much of his life as  a hermit also well known for poetry and  calligraphy but the quote that you  shared was  let me just make sure  i get this right  and this is ryukan referring to himself  last year a foolish monk period this  year no change period so when you're  judging yourself  harshly maybe just also remembering that  this this business of being human isn't  always easy even for people who focus on  cultivating a lot of these faculties  that's perfect last year a foolish monk  this year no change  got this  laughter in his spirit as a poet can be  thank you tim always  yes absolutely and i will include links  for everyone in the show notes to  everything we referred to so  jackcornfield.com  jackcornfield on twitter and instagram  jack underscore cornfield and for those  interested in the  lovingkindness meditations on your  meditations  section of your website which is  jackcornfield.com forward slash  meditations  i will also put some links to specific  meditations on that page in the show  notes at time blog  podcast  and thank you so much for taking the  time jack it's always such a pleasure  such a joy and so helpful to me  personally to spend time with you  well for me too i've enjoyed our  connection since the very beginning so  and it's good to see you you know  blossoming in your own unique tim way  thank you jack thank you i appreciate  that take care my friend thank you take  care jack be well bye  you

